//!
//! # Process Memory Mappings
//!
//! The regions a process adds to its address space at run time with `mmap`,
//! and their removal with `munmap`.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! Each process slot has a table of regions on the heap: a start, a length,
//! a protection and, for a file mapping, the file backing each. It starts at
//! 32 entries on the first `map` and doubles when full, up to `MAX_REGIONS`
//! (1024); `reset` frees it when the process ends. Every region is lazy:
//! `map` builds its page tables and reserves the frames its private pages
//! may need (`Mm::Pmm::reserve`), and a page gets its frame on first access,
//! from a page fault or a system call copy (`resolve`). `unmap` frees the
//! touched pages and gives back the reservation of the others. The
//! segments of the ELF image are file regions too, flagged as image (see
//! Program images); the stack is not a region: `unmap` never touches it.
//! The stack grows on demand: a fault anywhere in the 8 MiB below
//! `STACK_TOP` gets a zeroed page (`growStack`), and the unmapped gap below
//! `STACK_BOTTOM` ends a runaway recursion with SIGSEGV.
//!
//! ```text
//!   0x00007FFFFFFFF000  STACK_TOP
//!                       stack, grows down on demand
//!   0x00007FFFFF7FF000  STACK_BOTTOM = STACK_TOP - 8 MiB
//!                       (unmapped gap)
//!   0x00007F0000000000  MAP_TOP ── first region ends here
//!                       region 1  ▲ regions are handed out downwards,
//!                       region 2  │ one after the other, never reused
//!                       ...
//!   0x0000100000000000  MAP_BOTTOM: no region starts below this
//!                       (free)
//!   0x0000000000400000  ELF segments
//! ```
//!
//! The next address only moves down: a freed range is not handed out again.
//! The span holds about 110 TiB, so a process runs out of frames long before
//! it runs out of addresses.
//!
//! ## File regions
//!
//! A file mapping copies nothing at `map`: its pages come from the page
//! cache of the node (`Fs::Vfs::pageFrame`) when they are first touched. The
//! region holds the node (`Fs::Vfs::addMapping`, so the file cannot be
//! removed), the page-aligned file offset of its start, and `fileBytes`, the
//! bytes of the region that come from the file, fixed at `map` from the
//! file size then. A page is backed when it lies wholly within `fileBytes`.
//!
//! | Region            | Page         | Access        | Gets                                            |
//! |-------------------|--------------|---------------|-------------------------------------------------|
//! | anonymous         | any          | any           | a zeroed frame of the reservation               |
//! | private file      | backed       | read, fetch   | the cache frame, read-only (shared with every mapping) |
//! | private file      | backed       | write         | a copy of the cache page in a frame of the reservation, writable |
//! | private file      | not backed   | any           | a frame of the reservation: the file bytes, zeros after |
//! | shared file       | any          | any           | the cache frame, writable when the region is   |
//!
//! A write to a present read-only page of a writable private region (copy
//! on write) copies the cache page, maps the copy writable in place of the
//! cache frame, drops the reference on the cache frame and flushes the
//! local TLB entry: a process has one thread. M4 always copies, even when
//! the cache frame has one mapping. A `write` to the file changes the cache
//! frame, so an untouched or read-only page sees it and a copied page does
//! not, as on Linux. A shared region writes the cache frame itself, which
//! `read` sees; past the end of the file it reaches cache pages that `read`
//! never shows, zeroed at the fill and again when `truncate` cuts the file.
//!
//! ## Program images
//!
//! `Proc::Elf::load` makes each `PT_LOAD` segment a private file region of
//! the executable, flagged `image`: text and rodata read-only, so their
//! backed pages map the cache frames that every process of the program
//! shares, data writable, so a write copies its page, and the bss inside
//! the same region past its file bytes. An image region holds the node
//! twice: `Fs::Vfs::addMapping` (no removal) and `Fs::Vfs::addImage` (no
//! `write` or `truncate`, Linux `ETXTBSY`), both given back by `unmap` and
//! `teardown`. A writable shared region holds `Fs::Vfs::addSharedWriter`
//! the same way. The two exclude each other (Linux `ETXTBSY`, here `BUSY`):
//! `Syscall::Core::map` refuses a writable shared mapping of a file that is
//! a program image or being loaded, and `Proc::Elf::load` refuses a file
//! that has a writable shared mapping, since its stores would change the
//! text the program runs.
//!
//! The loader runs before the process has a slot, so it stages the regions
//! (`stageImage`, a `Proc::StagedImage`) with their node counts,
//! reservations and page tables taken; `Proc::Process::launch` adopts them
//! into the slot after `reset` (`adoptImage`), and every failure before
//! that gives them back once (`dropStaged`).
//!
//! ```text
//!   Proc::Elf::load ── stageImage per PT_LOAD ──► StagedImage { regions }
//!   Proc::Process::launch
//!     ├─ reset(slot)
//!     ├─ adoptImage(slot, staged)        regions copied into the slot
//!     ├─ spawnIn ok:   discardStaged     the slot owns the counts now
//!     └─ any failure:  reset, dropStaged  counts and reservations back
//! ```
//!
//! ## Accounting
//!
//! A page that may get a private frame is reserved at `map`: every page of
//! an anonymous region, every page of a writable private file region, and
//! only the pages that are not backed in a read-only private file region.
//! A shared region reserves nothing: cache frames do not count against free
//! frames, and a cache fill that finds no frame refuses the access (SIGSEGV
//! from a fault, `-EFAULT` from a system call copy). A refused `map` takes
//! nothing. The unused reservation of a region, returned by `unmap` and by
//! `teardown` at the end of a process, is its reserved pages minus the
//! pages that hold a private frame: a present page of an anonymous region,
//! a present writable page of a private region, or a present page that is
//! not backed.
//!
//! ## Locks
//!
//! Each process slot has an address space mutex (rank
//! `Lib::MutexRank::ADDRESS_SPACE`), since the threads of a process share
//! its regions and page tables. `map`, `unmap`, `resolve` (and `growStack`
//! under it), `teardown`, `reset` and `adoptImage` hold it while they read
//! or change the region table of the slot, so a table that grows is never
//! read half moved and a page is resolved by one thread at a time. Under
//! the lock a page found present is taken as it is: two threads that fault
//! on the same page both continue, and the page is mapped once.
//!
//! The node fields and the page cache are guarded by the VFS lock, which
//! ranks below: process table (10) ──► VFS (30) ──► address space (40).
//! `map` of a file runs with the VFS lock held by the caller. `unmap` and
//! `teardown` take it first, unless the running thread holds it already (a
//! system call that copies to user memory under it). `resolve` of a page
//! of a file region that finds the VFS lock not held drops the address
//! space lock, takes VFS, takes the address space lock again and looks the
//! region up again, since it may have changed meanwhile:
//!
//! ```text
//!   resolve(slot, page)
//!     ├─ lock space; region := lookup
//!     ├─ file region, VFS not held:  unlock space; lock VFS; lock space
//!     ├─ region := lookup again; present? done; else map the page once
//!     └─ unlock space; unlock VFS if taken here
//! ```
//!
//! A copy between the kernel and user memory (`Proc::User`) holds the
//! address space lock from the resolution of its range to the end of the
//! copy, so a sibling's `unmap` cannot free a frame it is about to touch.
//! It resolves through `resolveHeld`, which reports `NEEDS_VFS` instead of
//! dropping the lock itself; the copy then drops it, takes VFS, retakes it
//! and resolves the rest of its range again.
//!
//! None of them runs with a spinlock held. Before `initialize` (the boot
//! self-tests that run before the scheduler) nothing is locked.
//! `regionContaining` and `regionCount` read the table without the lock,
//! for callers that know nothing else changes it.
//!
//! ## Protection
//!
//! `READ`, `WRITE` and `EXECUTE` become page table bits: every region page is
//! user accessible (x86_64 cannot map a present page that ring 3 cannot
//! read), `WRITE` sets W, and no `EXECUTE` sets NX. A region without `READ`,
//! `WRITE` and `EXECUTE` (`PROT_NONE`) maps no page at all, so any access
//! faults. `WRITE` with `EXECUTE` is refused, the same rule as the ELF loader.
//!
//! ## Unmapping
//!
//! `unmap` frees every page of the range that lies in a region (a `PROT_NONE`
//! region has none, and is not walked) and trims the regions: a range that
//! covers a whole region removes it, one at an end shortens it, one in the
//! middle splits it in two (which needs a free table entry, and holds the
//! node once more). Pages outside every region are left alone.
//!
//! The functions take the process slot, the address space and ABI-neutral
//! arguments; `Syscall::Linux::mmap` translates the Linux flags.

import Boot from "@boot";
import Fs from "@fs";
import Lib from "@lib";
import Mm from "@mm";
import Sched from "@sched";

namespace Proc {
  /// One mapped region of a process.
  @cLayout
  record Region {
    /// The first byte, page aligned. 0 marks a free table entry.
    public start: u64;

    /// The length in bytes, a multiple of the page size.
    public length: u64;

    /// `Proc::Memory::READ`, `WRITE` and `EXECUTE` bits.
    public protection: u64;

    /// The file the region maps, null for an anonymous region. The region
    /// holds one `Fs::Vfs::addMapping` on it.
    public node: *mut Fs::Node;

    /// The file offset of `start`, page aligned.
    public offset: u64;

    /// The bytes of the region, from `start`, that come from the file; past
    /// them a private region gets zeroed pages. Fixed at `map`.
    public fileBytes: u64;

    /// 1 for a shared file region (`MAP_SHARED`), 0 otherwise. A writable
    /// one also holds one `Fs::Vfs::addSharedWriter` on `node`.
    public shared: u64;

    /// 1 for a segment of the program image, which also holds one
    /// `Fs::Vfs::addImage` on `node`; 0 otherwise.
    public image: u64;
  }

  /// The image regions `Proc::Elf::load` made for a process that has no
  /// slot yet. Each region holds its node counts, its reservation and its
  /// page tables; `Proc::Memory::adoptImage` hands them to a slot and
  /// `Proc::Memory::dropStaged` gives them back.
  @cLayout
  record StagedImage {
    /// The address of a heap array of `Proc::Memory::MAX_IMAGE_REGIONS`
    /// regions, 0 before the first `stageImage`.
    public table: u64;

    /// The regions staged.
    public count: u64;
  }
}

/// The region tables and the next address of every slot. Each size must
/// equal `MAX_SLOTS`. A slot's entries are guarded by its address space
/// mutex (`locks`), which every thread of the process, and the kernel
/// before the first starts and after the last ends (`launch`,
/// `finishThread`), takes through the functions below; the page table
/// edits under them take the page table spinlock.
record MemoryState {
  /// The region table of each slot: the address of a heap array of
  /// `capacities[s]` regions, 0 before the first `map`.
  static mut tables: u64[64];

  /// The number of regions each table holds: 0, then `INITIAL_REGIONS`,
  /// doubled as needed up to `MAX_REGIONS`.
  static mut capacities: u64[64];

  /// The end of the next region of each slot.
  static mut nextEnd: u64[64];

  /// The address space mutex of each slot, rank
  /// `Lib::MutexRank::ADDRESS_SPACE`.
  static mut locks: Sched::Mutex[64];

  /// True once `initialize` set the mutexes up: from then on the functions
  /// below take them.
  static mut locking: boolean;
}

/// The mapping API.
namespace Proc::Memory {
  /// The most regions a process can have.
  const MAX_REGIONS: u64 = 1024;

  /// The most image regions a program can have: one per `PT_LOAD`, which
  /// `Proc::Elf::MAX_PROGRAM_HEADERS` bounds. At most `INITIAL_REGIONS`, so
  /// adopting them grows the table of a fresh slot once.
  const MAX_IMAGE_REGIONS: u64 = 32;

  /// The regions a table starts with; it doubles when full.
  const INITIAL_REGIONS: u64 = 32;

  /// `reserveEntry` found no memory to grow the table.
  const NO_ROOM: u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;

  /// Process slots, `Proc::Process::MAX_PROCESSES` (this file cannot import
  /// `process.ign`, which imports it). A slot at or above it is refused.
  const MAX_SLOTS: u64 = 64;

  /// The first address past the user stack: `Mm::AddressSpace::USER_LIMIT`.
  /// The stack grows down from here, and the page at this address stays
  /// unmapped.
  const STACK_TOP: u64 = Mm::AddressSpace::USER_LIMIT;

  /// How far the stack may grow: 8 MiB, Linux's default limit.
  const STACK_LIMIT: u64 = 8388608;

  /// The lowest address the stack may reach. Nothing is mapped below it
  /// down to `MAP_TOP`, so running off the stack faults and kills.
  const STACK_BOTTOM: u64 = STACK_TOP - STACK_LIMIT;

  /// The end of the first region.
  const MAP_TOP: u64 = 0x00007F0000000000;

  /// No region starts below this.
  const MAP_BOTTOM: u64 = 0x0000100000000000;

  /// 4 KiB.
  const PAGE_SIZE: u64 = 4096;

  /// The region can be read.
  const READ: u64 = 1;

  /// The region can be written.
  const WRITE: u64 = 2;

  /// The region can be executed.
  const EXECUTE: u64 = 4;

  /// The operation succeeded.
  const OK: u64 = 0;

  /// A malformed request: a length of 0 or one that overflows, a start that
  /// is not page aligned, `WRITE` with `EXECUTE`, an unaligned file offset,
  /// or a shared region without a file.
  const INVALID: u64 = 1;

  /// No frame or no address left.
  const NO_MEMORY: u64 = 2;

  /// The region table of the process is full.
  const TOO_MANY: u64 = 3;

  /// `resolveHeld`: the access is not allowed or no frame is left.
  const REFUSED: u64 = 4;

  /// `resolveHeld`: the page is in a file region and the VFS lock is not
  /// held. The caller lets the address space lock go, takes VFS, takes the
  /// address space lock again and resolves again.
  const NEEDS_VFS: u64 = 5;

  /// Sets up the address space mutex of every slot. Called once the
  /// scheduler runs; until then nothing is locked.
  function initialize(): void {
    let mut slot: u64 = 0;

    while (slot < MAX_SLOTS) {
      Sched::Mutex::initialize(spaceLock(slot), Lib::MutexRank::ADDRESS_SPACE);
      slot += 1;
    }

    MemoryState::locking = true;
  }

  /// Takes the address space mutex of `slot` and returns true, or returns
  /// false before `initialize`. Pass the result to `unlockSpace`.
  function lockSpace(slot: u64): boolean {
    if (!MemoryState::locking) {
      return false;
    }

    Sched::Mutex::lock(spaceLock(slot));

    return true;
  }

  /// Ends a `lockSpace` of `slot` that returned `taken`.
  function unlockSpace(slot: u64, taken: boolean): void {
    if (taken) {
      Sched::Mutex::unlock(spaceLock(slot));
    }
  }

  /// The address space mutex of `slot`.
  function spaceLock(slot: u64): *mut Sched::Mutex {
    return &mut MemoryState::locks as *mut Sched::Mutex + slot;
  }

  /// Gives `slot` no regions and the first address, and frees its table.
  /// Called when a process starts in the slot and when it ends, after
  /// `teardown`: the regions must hold no node or reservation by then.
  function reset(slot: u64): void {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS) {
      return;
    }

    let taken: boolean = lockSpace(slot);

    clearSlot(slot);
    unlockSpace(slot, taken);
  }

  /// `reset` with the address space lock held.
  function clearSlot(slot: u64): void {
    if (*capacityOf(slot) > 0) {
      Mm::Heap::free(@pointerFromInteger<*mut u8>(*tableOf(slot)));
      *tableOf(slot) = 0;
      *capacityOf(slot) = 0;
    }

    *nextEndOf(slot) = MAP_TOP;
  }

  /// Maps a new lazy region of `length` bytes, rounded up to whole pages,
  /// with `protection` into `root`, the space of the process in `slot`, and
  /// writes its start to `address`. With `node` null the region is
  /// anonymous: its pages get zeroed frames on first access. Otherwise it
  /// maps file `node` from `offset` (page aligned), privately or `shared`;
  /// nothing is copied now, and the VFS lock must be held. The frames the
  /// region may need privately are reserved (see the module header).
  /// Returns `INVALID`, `NO_MEMORY` or `TOO_MANY`; a refused map takes no
  /// reservation and no node, and page tables built for it stay until the
  /// space is destroyed.
  function map(
    slot: u64,
    root: u64,
    length: u64,
    protection: u64,
    node: *mut Fs::Node,
    offset: u64,
    shared: boolean,
    address: *mut u64
  ): u64 {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS || length == 0 || length > MAP_TOP - MAP_BOTTOM || ((protection & WRITE) != 0 && (protection & EXECUTE) != 0)) {
      return INVALID;
    }

    if (offset % PAGE_SIZE != 0 || (shared && node == null)) {
      return INVALID;
    }

    let taken: boolean = lockSpace(slot);
    let status: u64 = mapLocked(slot, root, length, protection, node, offset, shared, address);

    unlockSpace(slot, taken);

    return status;
  }

  /// `map` with valid arguments and the address space lock held.
  function mapLocked(
    slot: u64,
    root: u64,
    length: u64,
    protection: u64,
    node: *mut Fs::Node,
    offset: u64,
    shared: boolean,
    address: *mut u64
  ): u64 {
    let rounded: u64 = (length + PAGE_SIZE - 1) / PAGE_SIZE * PAGE_SIZE;
    let end: u64 = *nextEndOf(slot);

    if (end - MAP_BOTTOM < rounded) {
      return NO_MEMORY;
    }

    let entry: u64 = reserveEntry(slot);

    if (entry == MAX_REGIONS) {
      return TOO_MANY;
    }

    if (entry == NO_ROOM) {
      return NO_MEMORY;
    }

    let start: u64 = end - rounded;
    let mut fileBytes: u64 = 0;

    if (node != null && offset < (*node).size) {
      fileBytes = (*node).size - offset < rounded ? (*node).size - offset : rounded;
    }

    let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionAt(slot, entry);

    (*region).length = rounded;
    (*region).protection = protection;
    (*region).node = node;
    (*region).offset = offset;
    (*region).fileBytes = fileBytes;
    (*region).shared = shared ? 1 : 0;
    (*region).image = 0;

    if (protection != 0) {
      let reserved: u64 = reservedPages(region, 0, rounded);

      // The reservation first: it bounds the request by the free memory
      // before any table is built, so a refused map takes nothing. The
      // tables then come from what is still available.
      if (!Mm::Pmm::reserve(reserved)) {
        return NO_MEMORY;
      }

      if (!Mm::AddressSpace::prepareRange(root, start, rounded)) {
        Mm::Pmm::unreserve(reserved);
        return NO_MEMORY;
      }
    }

    if (node != null) {
      Fs::Vfs::addMapping(node);

      if (isSharedWriter(region)) {
        Fs::Vfs::addSharedWriter(node);
      }
    }

    (*region).start = start;
    *nextEndOf(slot) = start;
    *address = start;

    return OK;
  }

  /// Removes `[address, address + length)`, `length` rounded up to whole
  /// pages, from the regions of `slot` and frees their pages in `root`.
  /// Returns `INVALID` for an unaligned address, a length of 0 or a range
  /// that leaves the user half, and `TOO_MANY` when splitting a region needs
  /// a table entry and none is free (nothing is changed then).
  function unmap(slot: u64, root: u64, address: u64, length: u64): u64 {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS || length == 0 || address % PAGE_SIZE != 0 || !Mm::AddressSpace::isUserRange(address, length)) {
      return INVALID;
    }

    let vfsTaken: boolean = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();
    let taken: boolean = lockSpace(slot);
    let status: u64 = unmapLocked(slot, root, address, length);

    unlockSpace(slot, taken);
    Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(vfsTaken);

    return status;
  }

  /// `unmap` with valid arguments, the VFS lock (once the scheduler runs)
  /// and the address space lock held.
  function unmapLocked(slot: u64, root: u64, address: u64, length: u64): u64 {
    let end: u64 = address + (length + PAGE_SIZE - 1) / PAGE_SIZE * PAGE_SIZE;

    // A split needs a free entry. Reserving it here, before the loop, may
    // move the table, which must not happen while the loop holds a region.
    if (needsSplit(slot, address, end)) {
      let spare: u64 = reserveEntry(slot);

      if (spare == MAX_REGIONS || spare == NO_ROOM) {
        return TOO_MANY;
      }
    }

    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < *capacityOf(slot)) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionAt(slot, index);
      let regionStart: u64 = (*region).start;
      let regionEnd: u64 = regionStart + (*region).length;

      if (regionStart != 0 && regionStart < end && address < regionEnd) {
        let cutStart: u64 = address > regionStart ? address : regionStart;
        let cutEnd: u64 = end < regionEnd ? end : regionEnd;

        // A PROT_NONE region maps no page, so there is nothing to free, and
        // walking a huge one page by page would stall the processor.
        if ((*region).protection != 0) {
          let reserved: u64 = reservedPages(region, cutStart - regionStart, cutEnd - regionStart);
          let privateFrames: u64 = releaseRange(region, root, cutStart, cutEnd);

          // The pages that never took their private frame still hold it.
          Mm::Pmm::unreserve(reserved - privateFrames);
        }

        trim(slot, region, cutStart, cutEnd);
      }

      index += 1;
    }

    return OK;
  }

  /// Takes `[cutStart, cutEnd)` out of `region`: removes it, shortens it
  /// or splits it, keeping the file offset and bytes of what stays and the
  /// node count right.
  function trim(slot: u64, region: *mut Proc::Region, cutStart: u64, cutEnd: u64): void {
    let regionStart: u64 = (*region).start;
    let regionEnd: u64 = regionStart + (*region).length;

    if (cutStart == regionStart && cutEnd == regionEnd) {
      (*region).start = 0;
      dropNode(region);
    } else if (cutStart == regionStart) {
      advance(region, cutEnd - regionStart);
      (*region).start = cutEnd;
      (*region).length = regionEnd - cutEnd;
    } else if (cutEnd == regionEnd) {
      (*region).length = cutStart - regionStart;
      capFileBytes(region);
    } else {
      let tail: *mut Proc::Region = regionAt(slot, freeEntry(slot));

      Lib::Mem::copy(tail as *mut u8, region as *u8, @sizeOf<Proc::Region>());
      advance(tail, cutEnd - regionStart);
      (*tail).start = cutEnd;
      (*tail).length = regionEnd - cutEnd;
      (*region).length = cutStart - regionStart;
      capFileBytes(region);

      if ((*tail).node != null) {
        let taken: boolean = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();

        Fs::Vfs::addMapping((*tail).node);

        if ((*tail).image != 0) {
          Fs::Vfs::addImage((*tail).node);
        }

        if (isSharedWriter(tail)) {
          Fs::Vfs::addSharedWriter((*tail).node);
        }

        Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(taken);
      }
    }
  }

  /// Moves the file view of `region` forward by `bytes` (a page multiple),
  /// for a region whose first `bytes` are cut.
  function advance(region: *mut Proc::Region, bytes: u64): void {
    (*region).offset += bytes;
    (*region).fileBytes = (*region).fileBytes > bytes ? (*region).fileBytes - bytes : 0;
  }

  /// Keeps the file bytes of `region` within its length.
  function capFileBytes(region: *mut Proc::Region): void {
    if ((*region).fileBytes > (*region).length) {
      (*region).fileBytes = (*region).length;
    }
  }

  /// Gives back the node of a removed region.
  function dropNode(region: *mut Proc::Region): void {
    if ((*region).node == null) {
      return;
    }

    let taken: boolean = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();

    Fs::Vfs::removeMapping((*region).node);

    if ((*region).image != 0) {
      Fs::Vfs::removeImage((*region).node);
    }

    if (isSharedWriter(region)) {
      Fs::Vfs::removeSharedWriter((*region).node);
    }

    Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(taken);
    (*region).node = null;
  }

  /// True when `region` is a writable shared file region, which holds one
  /// `Fs::Vfs::addSharedWriter` on its node.
  function isSharedWriter(region: *mut Proc::Region): boolean {
    return (*region).shared != 0 && ((*region).protection & WRITE) != 0;
  }

  /// Stages an image region of `length` bytes (a page multiple) at `start`
  /// in `root` for the program file `node`: `fileBytes` bytes from the page
  /// aligned file `offset`, then zeros, with `protection` (private). Takes
  /// the reservation of the pages that may get a private frame, builds the
  /// page tables and counts the region on the node; the VFS lock is held.
  /// Returns `NO_MEMORY` when no frame, table or heap is left, and
  /// `TOO_MANY` past `MAX_IMAGE_REGIONS`; nothing is taken then.
  function stageImage(
    staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage,
    root: u64,
    start: u64,
    length: u64,
    protection: u64,
    node: *mut Fs::Node,
    offset: u64,
    fileBytes: u64
  ): u64 {
    if ((*staged).count >= MAX_IMAGE_REGIONS) {
      return TOO_MANY;
    }

    if ((*staged).table == 0) {
      let table: *mut u8 = Mm::Heap::allocate(MAX_IMAGE_REGIONS * @sizeOf<Proc::Region>(), 8);

      if (table == null) {
        return NO_MEMORY;
      }

      (*staged).table = @integerFromPointer(table);
    }

    let region: *mut Proc::Region = stagedAt(staged, (*staged).count);

    (*region).start = start;
    (*region).length = length;
    (*region).protection = protection;
    (*region).node = node;
    (*region).offset = offset;
    (*region).fileBytes = fileBytes;
    (*region).shared = 0;
    (*region).image = 1;

    let reserved: u64 = reservedPages(region, 0, length);

    if (!Mm::Pmm::reserve(reserved)) {
      return NO_MEMORY;
    }

    if (!Mm::AddressSpace::prepareRange(root, start, length)) {
      Mm::Pmm::unreserve(reserved);
      return NO_MEMORY;
    }

    Fs::Vfs::addMapping(node);
    Fs::Vfs::addImage(node);
    (*staged).count += 1;

    return OK;
  }

  /// True when a staged region shares a page with `[start, end)`.
  function overlapsStaged(staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage, start: u64, end: u64): boolean {
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < (*staged).count) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = stagedAt(staged, index);

      if ((*region).start < end && start < (*region).start + (*region).length) {
        return true;
      }

      index += 1;
    }

    return false;
  }

  /// True when a staged region holds `address` and can be executed.
  function isStagedExecutable(staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage, address: u64): boolean {
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < (*staged).count) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = stagedAt(staged, index);

      if ((*region).start <= address && address - (*region).start < (*region).length) {
        return ((*region).protection & EXECUTE) != 0;
      }

      index += 1;
    }

    return false;
  }

  /// Copies the staged regions into `slot`, freshly `reset`, so its
  /// process resolves them. The staging still owns the counts: the caller
  /// ends it with `discardStaged` once the process exists, or with `reset`
  /// and `dropStaged` when it fails. Returns false when the heap cannot
  /// hold the region table; `slot` is unchanged then.
  function adoptImage(slot: u64, staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage): boolean {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS) {
      return false;
    }

    let taken: boolean = lockSpace(slot);
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < (*staged).count) {
      let entry: u64 = reserveEntry(slot);

      if (entry == MAX_REGIONS || entry == NO_ROOM) {
        clearSlot(slot);
        unlockSpace(slot, taken);
        return false;
      }

      Lib::Mem::copy(regionAt(slot, entry) as *mut u8, stagedAt(staged, index) as *u8, @sizeOf<Proc::Region>());
      index += 1;
    }

    unlockSpace(slot, taken);

    return true;
  }

  /// Frees the staging table once a slot owns its regions (`adoptImage`
  /// and the process exists).
  function discardStaged(staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage): void {
    if ((*staged).table != 0) {
      Mm::Heap::free(@pointerFromInteger<*mut u8>((*staged).table));
    }

    (*staged).table = 0;
    (*staged).count = 0;
  }

  /// Gives back what the staged regions hold, once: their reservation (no
  /// page of a staged region is present yet) and their node counts. Takes
  /// the VFS lock unless the caller holds it. The page tables go with the
  /// address space.
  function dropStaged(staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage): void {
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < (*staged).count) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = stagedAt(staged, index);

      Mm::Pmm::unreserve(reservedPages(region, 0, (*region).length));
      dropNode(region);
      index += 1;
    }

    discardStaged(staged);
  }

  /// Entry `index` of the staging table.
  function stagedAt(staged: *mut Proc::StagedImage, index: u64): *mut Proc::Region {
    return @pointerFromInteger<*mut Proc::Region>((*staged).table + index * @sizeOf<Proc::Region>());
  }

  /// Gives the page holding `address` its frame when a region of `slot`
  /// allows `access` there (`READ`, `WRITE` or `EXECUTE`), and copies a
  /// present read-only page of a writable private file region on a write.
  /// Returns true when the page is present afterwards with the access
  /// allowed, false when no region covers it, the region forbids the
  /// access, the page is present without it (a write to a read-only page
  /// of a region that does not allow it), or no frame is left for a cache
  /// page. Reading needs any access at all, since x86 cannot map a page
  /// ring 3 cannot read; `PROT_NONE` allows nothing. Runs in a thread of
  /// the process that owns `slot`, with no spinlock held: it takes the
  /// address space lock, and a file region the VFS lock (see Locks).
  function resolve(slot: u64, root: u64, address: u64, access: u64): boolean {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS) {
      return false;
    }

    let mut vfsTaken: boolean = false;
    let mut taken: boolean = lockSpace(slot);

    if (taken && !(address >= STACK_BOTTOM && address < STACK_TOP)) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionContaining(slot, address);

      // The VFS lock ranks below this one: let go, take both in order, and
      // let `resolveLocked` look the region up again.
      if (region != null && (*region).node != null && !Fs::Vfs::holdsLock()) {
        unlockSpace(slot, taken);
        vfsTaken = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();
        taken = lockSpace(slot);
      }
    }

    let resolved: boolean = resolveLocked(slot, root, address, access);

    unlockSpace(slot, taken);
    Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(vfsTaken);

    return resolved;
  }

  /// `resolve` for a caller that holds the address space lock of `slot`
  /// (`Proc::User` copies, which keep it to the end of the copy). Returns
  /// `OK`, `REFUSED`, or `NEEDS_VFS` without touching anything when the
  /// page is in a file region and the VFS lock, which ranks below, is not
  /// held.
  function resolveHeld(slot: u64, root: u64, address: u64, access: u64): u64 {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS) {
      return REFUSED;
    }

    if (MemoryState::locking && !(address >= STACK_BOTTOM && address < STACK_TOP)) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionContaining(slot, address);

      if (region != null && (*region).node != null && !Fs::Vfs::holdsLock()) {
        return NEEDS_VFS;
      }
    }

    return resolveLocked(slot, root, address, access) ? OK : REFUSED;
  }

  /// `resolve` with the address space lock held, and the VFS lock for a
  /// file region. A page found present is not mapped again.
  function resolveLocked(slot: u64, root: u64, address: u64, access: u64): boolean {
    if (address >= STACK_BOTTOM && address < STACK_TOP) {
      return growStack(root, address, access);
    }

    let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionContaining(slot, address);

    // A read needs any access at all; a write or a fetch needs its own bit.
    let needed: u64 = access & (WRITE | EXECUTE);

    if (region == null || (*region).protection == 0 || ((*region).protection & needed) != needed) {
      return false;
    }

    let page: u64 = address / PAGE_SIZE * PAGE_SIZE;
    let entry: u64 = Mm::Vmm::leafEntryIn(root, page);
    let present: boolean = (entry & Mm::Vmm::PRESENT) != 0;

    if (present && ((access & WRITE) == 0 || (entry & Mm::Vmm::WRITABLE) != 0)) {
      return true;
    }

    if ((*region).node == null) {
      if (present) {
        return false;
      }

      // The reservation made at `map` holds this page's frame, and the
      // tables were built then: neither step can fail.
      let frame: u64 = Mm::Pmm::allocateReserved();

      Lib::Mem::fill(Boot::Hhdm::toVirtual(frame), 0, PAGE_SIZE);
      Mm::AddressSpace::mapPrepared(root, page, frame, pageFlags((*region).protection));

      return true;
    }

    return resolveFile(region, root, page, access, present);
  }

  /// `resolve` for a page of a file region whose protection allows
  /// `access`. `present` is true for a write to a present read-only page:
  /// a private region copies it, a shared one refuses.
  function resolveFile(region: *mut Proc::Region, root: u64, page: u64, access: u64, present: boolean): boolean {
    let within: u64 = page - (*region).start;
    let flags: u64 = pageFlags((*region).protection);

    if ((*region).shared != 0) {
      if (present) {
        return false;
      }

      return mapCacheFrame(region, root, page, flags);
    }

    if (isBacked(region, within) && (access & WRITE) == 0) {
      return mapCacheFrame(region, root, page, flags & ~Mm::Vmm::WRITABLE);
    }

    // A private page: the reservation holds its frame. Its bytes are the
    // file's, as the cache or `data` has them now, and zeros after the
    // file bytes of the region.
    let frame: u64 = Mm::Pmm::allocateReserved();
    let bytes: *mut u8 = Boot::Hhdm::toVirtual(frame);

    Lib::Mem::fill(bytes, 0, PAGE_SIZE);

    if ((*region).fileBytes > within) {
      let wanted: u64 = (*region).fileBytes - within < PAGE_SIZE ? (*region).fileBytes - within : PAGE_SIZE;
      let mut copied: u64 = 0;
      let taken: boolean = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();

      Fs::Vfs::read((*region).node, (*region).offset + within, bytes, wanted, &mut copied as *mut u64);
      Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(taken);
    }

    // Copy on write: the copy takes the place of the cache frame, whose
    // reference goes back; `freePage` drops the local TLB entry. Another
    // processor running a sibling thread may still hold it until the TLB
    // shootdown exists (C4 T4).
    if (present) {
      Mm::AddressSpace::freePage(root, page);
    }

    Mm::AddressSpace::mapPrepared(root, page, frame, flags);

    return true;
  }

  /// Maps the cache frame of `page` of a file region with `flags`, taking a
  /// reference for the mapping. Returns false when no frame or heap is left
  /// for the cache.
  function mapCacheFrame(region: *mut Proc::Region, root: u64, page: u64, flags: u64): boolean {
    let taken: boolean = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();
    let frame: u64 = Fs::Vfs::pageFrame((*region).node, ((*region).offset + page - (*region).start) / PAGE_SIZE);

    if (frame != Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME) {
      Mm::Pmm::reference(frame);
    }

    Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(taken);

    if (frame == Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME) {
      return false;
    }

    Mm::AddressSpace::mapPrepared(root, page, frame, flags);

    return true;
  }

  /// True when the page at `within` bytes into `region` lies wholly within
  /// its file bytes. An anonymous region has none.
  function isBacked(region: *mut Proc::Region, within: u64): boolean {
    return within + PAGE_SIZE <= (*region).fileBytes;
  }

  /// The pages of `[firstByte, endByte)` bytes into `region` that may take a private
  /// frame, so `map` reserved them: every page of an anonymous or writable
  /// private region, the pages that are not backed in a read-only private
  /// one, none of a shared or `PROT_NONE` region.
  function reservedPages(region: *mut Proc::Region, firstByte: u64, endByte: u64): u64 {
    if ((*region).protection == 0 || (*region).shared != 0) {
      return 0;
    }

    if (((*region).protection & WRITE) != 0 || (*region).fileBytes <= firstByte) {
      return (endByte - firstByte) / PAGE_SIZE;
    }

    let mut count: u64 = 0;
    let mut within: u64 = firstByte;

    while (within < endByte) {
      if (!isBacked(region, within)) {
        count += 1;
      }

      within += PAGE_SIZE;
    }

    return count;
  }

  /// True when the present page with the leaf `entry`, `within` bytes into
  /// `region`, holds a frame of the region's reservation rather than a
  /// cache frame.
  function isPrivateFrame(region: *mut Proc::Region, within: u64, entry: u64): boolean {
    if ((*region).shared != 0) {
      return false;
    }

    return (*region).node == null || (entry & Mm::Vmm::WRITABLE) != 0 || !isBacked(region, within);
  }

  /// Unmaps every present page of `[firstByte, endByte)` in `region`, dropping its
  /// frame or its reference on a cache frame, and returns how many held a
  /// private frame.
  function releaseRange(region: *mut Proc::Region, root: u64, firstByte: u64, endByte: u64): u64 {
    let mut page: u64 = firstByte;
    let mut privateFrames: u64 = 0;

    while (page < endByte) {
      let entry: u64 = Mm::Vmm::leafEntryIn(root, page);

      if ((entry & Mm::Vmm::PRESENT) != 0 && Mm::AddressSpace::freePage(root, page) && isPrivateFrame(region, page - (*region).start, entry)) {
        privateFrames += 1;
      }

      page += PAGE_SIZE;
    }

    return privateFrames;
  }

  /// The private frames of the present pages of `region`.
  function privateFrameCount(region: *mut Proc::Region, root: u64): u64 {
    let mut count: u64 = 0;
    let mut within: u64 = 0;

    while (within < (*region).length) {
      let entry: u64 = Mm::Vmm::leafEntryIn(root, (*region).start + within);

      if ((entry & Mm::Vmm::PRESENT) != 0 && isPrivateFrame(region, within, entry)) {
        count += 1;
      }

      within += PAGE_SIZE;
    }

    return count;
  }

  /// Gives the stack page holding `address` a zeroed frame. The stack can be
  /// read and written, never executed. Its frames are not reserved ahead,
  /// as on Linux: when none is left the access is refused and the process
  /// is killed with SIGSEGV. The page tables were built when the process
  /// started (`prepareStack`), so only the frame can be missing. The
  /// address space lock is held (`resolve`), so a present page is final.
  function growStack(root: u64, address: u64, access: u64): boolean {
    if ((access & EXECUTE) != 0) {
      return false;
    }

    let page: u64 = address / PAGE_SIZE * PAGE_SIZE;

    if ((Mm::Vmm::leafEntryIn(root, page) & Mm::Vmm::PRESENT) != 0) {
      return true;
    }

    let frame: u64 = Mm::Pmm::allocateFrame();

    if (frame == Mm::Pmm::NO_FRAME) {
      return false;
    }

    Lib::Mem::fill(Boot::Hhdm::toVirtual(frame), 0, PAGE_SIZE);
    Mm::AddressSpace::mapPrepared(root, page, frame, Mm::Vmm::WRITABLE | Mm::Vmm::NO_EXECUTE);

    return true;
  }

  /// Builds the page tables of the whole stack range of `root`, so that
  /// growing the stack later needs only the frames of its pages. Returns
  /// false when no frame is available for a table.
  function prepareStack(root: u64): boolean {
    return Mm::AddressSpace::prepareRange(root, STACK_BOTTOM, STACK_LIMIT);
  }

  /// The region of `slot` that holds `address`, or null.
  function regionContaining(slot: u64, address: u64): *mut Proc::Region {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS) {
      return null;
    }

    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < *capacityOf(slot)) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionAt(slot, index);

      if ((*region).start != 0 && (*region).start <= address && address - (*region).start < (*region).length) {
        return region;
      }

      index += 1;
    }

    return null;
  }

  /// The number of regions `slot` has.
  function regionCount(slot: u64): u64 {
    let mut count: u64 = 0;
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < *capacityOf(slot)) {
      if ((*regionAt(slot, index)).start != 0) {
        count += 1;
      }

      index += 1;
    }

    return count;
  }

  /// True when `[start, end)` falls strictly inside one region, so removing
  /// it leaves a piece on each side.
  function needsSplit(slot: u64, start: u64, end: u64): boolean {
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < *capacityOf(slot)) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionAt(slot, index);

      if ((*region).start != 0 && (*region).start < start && end < (*region).start + (*region).length) {
        return true;
      }

      index += 1;
    }

    return false;
  }

  /// Ends the regions of `slot` when its process ends: gives back the
  /// unused reservation of each region and the node of each file region.
  /// The address space `root` is destroyed later with the frames it maps,
  /// which drops the references on cache frames. Takes the VFS lock unless
  /// the caller holds it, then the address space lock; no spinlock may be
  /// held.
  function teardown(slot: u64, root: u64): void {
    if (slot >= MAX_SLOTS) {
      return;
    }

    let vfsTaken: boolean = Fs::Vfs::lockUnlessHeld();
    let taken: boolean = lockSpace(slot);

    teardownLocked(slot, root);
    unlockSpace(slot, taken);
    Fs::Vfs::unlockIfTaken(vfsTaken);
  }

  /// `teardown` with both locks held.
  function teardownLocked(slot: u64, root: u64): void {
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < *capacityOf(slot)) {
      let region: *mut Proc::Region = regionAt(slot, index);

      if ((*region).start != 0) {
        if ((*region).protection != 0) {
          Mm::Pmm::unreserve(reservedPages(region, 0, (*region).length) - privateFrameCount(region, root));
        }

        dropNode(region);
        (*region).start = 0;
      }

      index += 1;
    }
  }

  /// The page table flags of a protection: W for `WRITE`, NX unless
  /// `EXECUTE`. The U/S bit is added by `Mm::AddressSpace`.
  function pageFlags(protection: u64): u64 {
    let mut flags: u64 = 0;

    if ((protection & WRITE) != 0) {
      flags = flags | Mm::Vmm::WRITABLE;
    }

    if ((protection & EXECUTE) == 0) {
      flags = flags | Mm::Vmm::NO_EXECUTE;
    }

    return flags;
  }

  /// The first free entry of `slot`, growing the table when every entry is
  /// taken: `MAX_REGIONS` when it is at its limit and full, `NO_ROOM` when the
  /// heap cannot hold a bigger one.
  function reserveEntry(slot: u64): u64 {
    let capacity: u64 = *capacityOf(slot);
    let entry: u64 = freeEntry(slot);

    if (entry < capacity) {
      return entry;
    }

    if (capacity >= MAX_REGIONS) {
      return MAX_REGIONS;
    }

    let grown: u64 = capacity == 0 ? INITIAL_REGIONS : capacity * 2;
    let bytes: u64 = grown * @sizeOf<Proc::Region>();
    let table: *mut u8 = Mm::Heap::allocate(bytes, 8);

    if (table == null) {
      return NO_ROOM;
    }

    Lib::Mem::fill(table, 0, bytes);

    if (capacity > 0) {
      Lib::Mem::copy(table, @pointerFromInteger<*u8>(*tableOf(slot)), capacity * @sizeOf<Proc::Region>());
      Mm::Heap::free(@pointerFromInteger<*mut u8>(*tableOf(slot)));
    }

    *tableOf(slot) = @integerFromPointer(table);
    *capacityOf(slot) = grown;

    return capacity;
  }

  /// The first free entry of `slot`, or its capacity when none is free.
  function freeEntry(slot: u64): u64 {
    let mut index: u64 = 0;

    while (index < *capacityOf(slot) && (*regionAt(slot, index)).start != 0) {
      index += 1;
    }

    return index;
  }

  /// Entry `index` of the table of `slot`.
  function regionAt(slot: u64, index: u64): *mut Proc::Region {
    return @pointerFromInteger<*mut Proc::Region>(*tableOf(slot) + index * @sizeOf<Proc::Region>());
  }

  /// The address of the table address of `slot`.
  function tableOf(slot: u64): *mut u64 {
    return @pointerFromInteger<*mut u64>(@integerFromPointer(&mut MemoryState::tables as *mut u64) + slot * 8);
  }

  /// The address of the region count of `slot`.
  function capacityOf(slot: u64): *mut u64 {
    return @pointerFromInteger<*mut u64>(@integerFromPointer(&mut MemoryState::capacities as *mut u64) + slot * 8);
  }

  /// The next end address of `slot`.
  function nextEndOf(slot: u64): *mut u64 {
    return @pointerFromInteger<*mut u64>(@integerFromPointer(&mut MemoryState::nextEnd as *mut u64) + slot * 8);
  }
}
